claim.claim-text:
28. Apparatus for conveying containers, said apparatus comprising: continuous motion first and second transfer conveyors, the first conveyor being rotatable about a respective first axis, the second conveyor being rotatable about a respective second axis and the axes being generally parallel and laterally spaced; the conveyors being so sized and their axes being so placed that a portion of the first and second conveyors axially 

overlap and are axially spaced from each other and rotate past each other about their respective axes at an overlapping region of both of the first and second conveyors; the first conveyor having a first surface, the second conveyor having a second surface, and the first and second surfaces being opposed to each other at the region where the first and second conveyors axially overlap and are axially spaced apart; the first surface being operable to apply a first attractive force for holding containers to the first surface, the second surface being operable to apply a second attractive force for holding containers to the second surface; a continuous motion mandrel carrier rotatable about a third axis spaced from the first axis, the first and third axes being so positioned and the mandrel carrier and the first conveyor being of such shape and size and so placed that containers are transferred in a single row from the mandrel carrier to the first surface of the first conveyor; a continuous motion belt conveyor including a flight section movable to convey containers away from the second conveyor, the flight section being positioned to receive cans from the second conveyor at a second transfer location downstream in the rotation of the second conveyor from the region where the first and second conveyors overlap; the second surface of the second conveyor including concentric outer and inner tracks formed around the second axis, each of the outer and inner tracks being adapted to provide a second attractive force for holding containers to the respective one of the outer and inner tracks when the containers are transferred to the second surface of the second conveyor from the first surface of the first conveyor; the path of containers on the first surface of the first conveyor, the inner and outer tracks on the second conveyor and the overlapping region all being so placed on the respective conveyors that in the region where the first and second conveyors overlap as they rotate, the path of some of the containers on the first conveyor first cross in front of the outer track on the second conveyor and then intersect the inner track of the second conveyor; 

 as the first and second conveyors rotate, and as a first plurality of the containers supported on the first conveyor cross the outer track of the second conveyor, the first conveyor is operative to hold the first plurality of containers to the first conveyor as the first plurality of containers pass the outer track of the second conveyor and the first conveyor is operative to release the first plurality of containers to the inner track of the second conveyor when the first plurality of containers are at the inner track of the second conveyor; the first and second conveyors being operative to act on a second plurality of the containers supported on the first conveyor such that as each of the second plurality of containers crosses the outer track of the second conveyor, the first conveyor is operative to discontinue the first attractive force and release the second plurality of containers to the second attractive force at the outer track of the second conveyor; whereby the first and second pluralities of containers are transported on the inner and outer tracks respectively to the second transfer location at the belt.
